#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2016
msgid "Text Sequence Type --- :class:`str`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2018
msgid ""
"Textual data in Python is handled with :class:`str` objects, or "
":dfn:`strings`. Strings are immutable :ref:`sequences <typesseq>` of "
"Unicode code points.  String literals are written in a variety of ways:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2023
msgid "Single quotes: ``'allows embedded \"double\" quotes'``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2024
msgid "Double quotes: ``\"allows embedded 'single' quotes\"``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2025
msgid ""
"Triple quoted: ``'''Three single quotes'''``, ``\"\"\"Three double "
"quotes\"\"\"``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2027
msgid ""
"Triple quoted strings may span multiple lines - all associated whitespace"
" will be included in the string literal."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2030
msgid ""
"String literals that are part of a single expression and have only "
"whitespace between them will be implicitly converted to a single string "
"literal. That is, ``(\"spam \" \"eggs\") == \"spam eggs\"``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2034
msgid ""
"See :ref:`strings` for more about the various forms of string literal, "
"including supported :ref:`escape sequences <escape-sequences>`, and the "
"``r`` (\"raw\") prefix that disables most escape sequence processing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2038
msgid ""
"Strings may also be created from other objects using the :class:`str` "
"constructor."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2041
msgid ""
"Since there is no separate \"character\" type, indexing a string produces"
" strings of length 1. That is, for a non-empty string *s*, ``s[0] == "
"s[0:1]``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2047
msgid ""
"There is also no mutable string type, but :meth:`str.join` or "
":class:`io.StringIO` can be used to efficiently construct strings from "
"multiple fragments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2051
msgid ""
"For backwards compatibility with the Python 2 series, the ``u`` prefix is"
" once again permitted on string literals. It has no effect on the meaning"
" of string literals and cannot be combined with the ``r`` prefix."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2065
msgid ""
"Return a :ref:`string <textseq>` version of *object*.  If *object* is not"
" provided, returns the empty string.  Otherwise, the behavior of "
"``str()`` depends on whether *encoding* or *errors* is given, as follows."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2069
msgid ""
"If neither *encoding* nor *errors* is given, ``str(object)`` returns "
":meth:`type(object).__str__(object) <object.__str__>`, which is the "
"\"informal\" or nicely printable string representation of *object*.  For "
"string objects, this is the string itself.  If *object* does not have a "
":meth:`~object.__str__` method, then :func:`str` falls back to returning "
":func:`repr(object) <repr>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2081
msgid ""
"If at least one of *encoding* or *errors* is given, *object* should be a "
":term:`bytes-like object` (e.g. :class:`bytes` or :class:`bytearray`).  "
"In this case, if *object* is a :class:`bytes` (or :class:`bytearray`) "
"object, then ``str(bytes, encoding, errors)`` is equivalent to "
":meth:`bytes.decode(encoding, errors) <bytes.decode>`.  Otherwise, the "
"bytes object underlying the buffer object is obtained before calling "
":meth:`bytes.decode`.  See :ref:`binaryseq` and :ref:`bufferobjects` for "
"information on buffer objects."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2090
msgid ""
"Passing a :class:`bytes` object to :func:`str` without the *encoding* or "
"*errors* arguments falls under the first case of returning the informal "
"string representation (see also the :option:`-b` command-line option to "
"Python).  For example::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2095
msgid ""
">>> str(b'Zoot!')\n"
"\"b'Zoot!'\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2098
msgid ""
"For more information on the ``str`` class and its methods, see "
":ref:`textseq` and the :ref:`string-methods` section below.  To output "
"formatted strings, see the :ref:`f-strings` and :ref:`formatstrings` "
"sections.  In addition, see the :ref:`stringservices` section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2110
msgid "String Methods"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2115
msgid ""
"Strings implement all of the :ref:`common <typesseq-common>` sequence "
"operations, along with the additional methods described below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2118
msgid ""
"Strings also support two styles of string formatting, one providing a "
"large degree of flexibility and customization (see :meth:`str.format`, "
":ref:`formatstrings` and :ref:`string-formatting`) and the other based on"
" C ``printf`` style formatting that handles a narrower range of types and"
" is slightly harder to use correctly, but is often faster for the cases "
"it can handle (:ref:`old-string-formatting`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2125
msgid ""
"The :ref:`textservices` section of the standard library covers a number "
"of other modules that provide various text related utilities (including "
"regular expression support in the :mod:`re` module)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2131
msgid ""
"Return a copy of the string with its first character capitalized and the "
"rest lowercased. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2134
msgid ""
">>> 'PYTHON IS AMAZING'.capitalize()\n"
"'Python is amazing'\n"
">>> 'ǋemačka starts With a non-ASCII digraph'.capitalize()\n"
"'ǋemačka starts with a non-ascii digraph'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2141 ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2607
msgid "See also :meth:`title`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2143
msgid ""
"The first character is now put into titlecase rather than uppercase. This"
" means that characters like digraphs will only have their first letter "
"capitalized, instead of the full character."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2151
msgid ""
"Return a casefolded copy of the string. Casefolded strings may be used "
"for caseless matching."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2154
msgid ""
"Casefolding is similar to lowercasing but more aggressive because it is "
"intended to remove all case distinctions in a string. For example, the "
"German lowercase letter ``'ß'`` is equivalent to ``\"ss\"``. Since it is "
"already lowercase, :meth:`lower` would do nothing to ``'ß'``; "
":meth:`casefold` converts it to ``\"ss\"``. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2161
msgid ""
">>> 'straße'.lower()\n"
"'straße'\n"
">>> 'straße'.casefold()\n"
"'strasse'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2168
msgid ""
"The casefolding algorithm is `described in section 3.13.3 'Default Case "
"Folding' of the Unicode Standard "
"<https://www.unicode.org/versions/Unicode17.0.0/core-"
"spec/chapter-3/#G53253>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2177
msgid ""
"Return centered in a string of length *width*. Padding is done using the "
"specified *fillchar* (default is an ASCII space). The original string is "
"returned if *width* is less than or equal to ``len(s)``.  For example::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2181
msgid ""
">>> 'Python'.center(10)\n"
"'  Python  '\n"
">>> 'Python'.center(10, '-')\n"
"'--Python--'\n"
">>> 'Python'.center(4)\n"
"'Python'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2191
msgid ""
"Return the number of non-overlapping occurrences of substring *sub* in "
"the range [*start*, *end*].  Optional arguments *start* and *end* are "
"interpreted as in slice notation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2195
msgid ""
"If *sub* is empty, returns the number of empty strings between characters"
" which is the length of the string plus one. For example::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2198
msgid ""
">>> 'spam, spam, spam'.count('spam')\n"
"3\n"
">>> 'spam, spam, spam'.count('spam', 5)\n"
"2\n"
">>> 'spam, spam, spam'.count('spam', 5, 10)\n"
"1\n"
">>> 'spam, spam, spam'.count('eggs')\n"
"0\n"
">>> 'spam, spam, spam'.count('')\n"
"17"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2211
msgid "Return the string encoded to :class:`bytes`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2213 ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3962
msgid ""
"*encoding* defaults to ``'utf-8'``; see :ref:`standard-encodings` for "
"possible values."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2216
msgid ""
"*errors* controls how encoding errors are handled. If ``'strict'`` (the "
"default), a :exc:`UnicodeError` exception is raised. Other possible "
"values are ``'ignore'``, ``'replace'``, ``'xmlcharrefreplace'``, "
"``'backslashreplace'`` and any other name registered via "
":func:`codecs.register_error`. See :ref:`error-handlers` for details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2223
msgid ""
"For performance reasons, the value of *errors* is not checked for "
"validity unless an encoding error actually occurs, :ref:`devmode` is "
"enabled or a :ref:`debug build <debug-build>` is used. For example::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2229
msgid ""
">>> encoded_str_to_bytes = 'Python'.encode()\n"
">>> type(encoded_str_to_bytes)\n"
"<class 'bytes'>\n"
">>> encoded_str_to_bytes\n"
"b'Python'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2236 ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3981
msgid "Added support for keyword arguments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2239 ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3984
msgid ""
"The value of the *errors* argument is now checked in :ref:`devmode` and "
"in :ref:`debug mode <debug-build>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2246
msgid ""
"Return ``True`` if the string ends with the specified *suffix*, otherwise"
" return ``False``.  *suffix* can also be a tuple of suffixes to look for."
"  With optional *start*, test beginning at that position.  With optional "
"*end*, stop comparing at that position. Using *start* and *end* is "
"equivalent to ``str[start:end].endswith(suffix)``. For example::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2252
msgid ""
">>> 'Python'.endswith('on')\n"
"True\n"
">>> 'a tuple of suffixes'.endswith(('at', 'in'))\n"
"False\n"
">>> 'a tuple of suffixes'.endswith(('at', 'es'))\n"
"True\n"
">>> 'Python is amazing'.endswith('is', 0, 9)\n"
"True"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2261
msgid "See also :meth:`startswith` and :meth:`removesuffix`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2266
msgid ""
"Return a copy of the string where all tab characters are replaced by one "
"or more spaces, depending on the current column and the given tab size.  "
"Tab positions occur every *tabsize* characters (default is 8, giving tab "
"positions at columns 0, 8, 16 and so on).  To expand the string, the "
"current column is set to zero and the string is examined character by "
"character.  If the character is a tab (``\\t``), one or more space "
"characters are inserted in the result until the current column is equal "
"to the next tab position. (The tab character itself is not copied.)  If "
"the character is a newline (``\\n``) or return (``\\r``), it is copied "
"and the current column is reset to zero.  Any other character is copied "
"unchanged and the current column is incremented by one regardless of how "
"the character is represented when printed. For example::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2279
msgid ""
">>> '01\\t012\\t0123\\t01234'.expandtabs()\n"
"'01      012     0123    01234'\n"
">>> '01\\t012\\t0123\\t01234'.expandtabs(4)\n"
"'01  012 0123    01234'\n"
">>> print('01\\t012\\n0123\\t01234'.expandtabs(4))\n"
"01  012\n"
"0123    01234"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2290
msgid ""
"Return the lowest index in the string where substring *sub* is found "
"within the slice ``s[start:end]``.  Optional arguments *start* and *end* "
"are interpreted as in slice notation.  Return ``-1`` if *sub* is not "
"found. For example::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2295
msgid ""
">>> 'spam, spam, spam'.find('sp')\n"
"0\n"
">>> 'spam, spam, spam'.find('sp', 5)\n"
"6"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2300
msgid "See also :meth:`rfind` and :meth:`index`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2304
msgid ""
"The :meth:`~str.find` method should be used only if you need to know the "
"position of *sub*.  To check if *sub* is a substring or not, use the "
":keyword:`in` operator::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2308
msgid ""
">>> 'Py' in 'Python'\n"
"True"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2314
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Perform a string formatting operation.  The string on which this method "
"is called can contain literal text or replacement fields delimited by "
"braces ``{}``.  Each replacement field refers to a positional argument "
"(numbered explicitly or automatically) or a keyword argument.  Returns a "
"copy of the string where each replacement field is replaced with the "
"string value of the corresponding argument. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2321
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
">>> \"The sum of {} + {} is {}\".format(1, 2, 1+2)\n"
"'The sum of 1 + 2 is 3'\n"
">>> \"{1} expects the {0} Inquisition!\".format(\"Spanish\", \"Nobody\")\n"
"'Nobody expects the Spanish Inquisition!'\n"
">>> \"The sum of {a} + {b} is {answer}\".format(answer=1+2, a=1, b=2)\n"
"'The sum of 1 + 2 is 3'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2330
msgid ""
"See :ref:`formatstrings` for a description of the various formatting "
"options that can be specified in format strings."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2334
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"When formatting a number (:class:`int`, :class:`float`, :class:`complex`,"
" :class:`decimal.Decimal` and subclasses) with the ``n`` type (ex: "
"``'{:n}'.format(1234)``), the function temporarily sets the ``LC_CTYPE`` "
"locale to the ``LC_NUMERIC`` locale to decode ``decimal_point`` and "
"``thousands_sep`` fields of :c:func:`localeconv` if they are non-ASCII or"
" longer than 1 byte, and the ``LC_NUMERIC`` locale is different than the "
"``LC_CTYPE`` locale.  This temporary change affects other threads."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2343
msgid ""
"When formatting a number with the ``n`` type, the function sets "
"temporarily the ``LC_CTYPE`` locale to the ``LC_NUMERIC`` locale in some "
"cases."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2351
msgid ""
"Similar to ``str.format(**mapping)``, except that ``mapping`` is used "
"directly and not copied to a :class:`dict`.  This is useful if for "
"example ``mapping`` is a dict subclass:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2367
msgid ""
"Like :meth:`~str.find`, but raise :exc:`ValueError` when the substring is"
" not found. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2370
msgid ""
">>> 'spam, spam, spam'.index('spam')\n"
"0\n"
">>> 'spam, spam, spam'.index('eggs')\n"
"Traceback (most recent call last):\n"
"  File \"<python-input-0>\", line 1, in <module>\n"
"    'spam, spam, spam'.index('eggs')\n"
"    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~^^^^^^^^\n"
"ValueError: substring not found"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2381
msgid "See also :meth:`rindex`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2386
msgid ""
"Return ``True`` if all characters in the string are alphanumeric and "
"there is at least one character, ``False`` otherwise.  A character ``c`` "
"is alphanumeric if one of the following returns ``True``: "
"``c.isalpha()``, ``c.isdecimal()``, ``c.isdigit()``, or "
"``c.isnumeric()``. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2391
msgid ""
">>> 'abc123'.isalnum()\n"
"True\n"
">>> 'abc123!@#'.isalnum()\n"
"False\n"
">>> ''.isalnum()\n"
"False\n"
">>> ' '.isalnum()\n"
"False"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2405
msgid ""
"Return ``True`` if all characters in the string are alphabetic and there "
"is at least one character, ``False`` otherwise.  Alphabetic characters "
"are those characters defined in the Unicode character database as "
"\"Letter\", i.e., those with general category property being one of "
"\"Lm\", \"Lt\", \"Lu\", \"Ll\", or \"Lo\".  Note that this is different "
"from the `Alphabetic property defined in section 4.10 'Letters, "
"Alphabetic, and Ideographic' of the Unicode Standard "
"<https://www.unicode.org/versions/Unicode17.0.0/core-"
"spec/chapter-4/#G91002>`__. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2414
msgid ""
">>> 'Letters and spaces'.isalpha()\n"
"False\n"
">>> 'LettersOnly'.isalpha()\n"
"True\n"
">>> 'µ'.isalpha()  # non-ASCII characters can be considered alphabetical "
"too\n"
"True"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2423
msgid "See :ref:`unicode-properties`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2428
msgid ""
"Return ``True`` if the string is empty or all characters in the string "
"are ASCII, ``False`` otherwise. ASCII characters have code points in the "
"range U+0000-U+007F. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2432
msgid ""
">>> 'ASCII characters'.isascii()\n"
"True\n"
">>> 'µ'.isascii()\n"
"False"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2444
msgid ""
"Return ``True`` if all characters in the string are decimal characters "
"and there is at least one character, ``False`` otherwise. Decimal "
"characters are those that can be used to form numbers in base 10, such as"
" U+0660, ARABIC-INDIC DIGIT ZERO.  Formally a decimal character is a "
"character in the Unicode General Category \"Nd\". For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2451
msgid ""
">>> '0123456789'.isdecimal()\n"
"True\n"
">>> '٠١٢٣٤٥٦٧٨٩'.isdecimal()  # Arabic-Indic digits zero to nine\n"
"True\n"
">>> 'alphabetic'.isdecimal()\n"
"False"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2463
msgid ""
"Return ``True`` if all characters in the string are digits and there is "
"at least one character, ``False`` otherwise.  Digits include decimal "
"characters and digits that need special handling, such as the "
"compatibility superscript digits. This covers digits which cannot be used"
" to form numbers in base 10, like the `Kharosthi numbers "
"<https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kharosthi#Numerals>`__. Formally, a digit "
"is a character that has the property value Numeric_Type=Digit or "
"Numeric_Type=Decimal."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2471 ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2552
#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2569 ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2596
#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2651 ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2728
#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2839 ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2860
#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2925 ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2946
#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2960 ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3047
#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3071 ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3085
#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3200
msgid "For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2473
msgid ""
">>> '0123456789'.isdigit()\n"
"True\n"
">>> '٠١٢٣٤٥٦٧٨٩'.isdigit()  # Arabic-Indic digits zero to nine\n"
"True\n"
">>> '⅕'.isdigit()  # Vulgar fraction one fifth\n"
"False\n"
">>> '²'.isdecimal(), '²'.isdigit(),  '²'.isnumeric()\n"
"(False, True, True)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2484
msgid "See also :meth:`isdecimal` and :meth:`isnumeric`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2489
msgid ""
"Return ``True`` if the string is a valid identifier according to the "
"language definition, section :ref:`identifiers`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2492
msgid ""
":func:`keyword.iskeyword` can be used to test whether string ``s`` is a "
"reserved identifier, such as :keyword:`def` and :keyword:`class`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2495
msgid "Example: ::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2498
msgid ""
">>> from keyword import iskeyword\n"
"\n"
">>> 'hello'.isidentifier(), iskeyword('hello')\n"
"(True, False)\n"
">>> 'def'.isidentifier(), iskeyword('def')\n"
"(True, True)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2508
msgid ""
"Return ``True`` if all cased characters [4]_ in the string are lowercase "
"and there is at least one cased character, ``False`` otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2514
msgid ""
"Return ``True`` if all characters in the string are numeric characters, "
"and there is at least one character, ``False`` otherwise. Numeric "
"characters include digit characters, and all characters that have the "
"Unicode numeric value property, e.g. U+2155, VULGAR FRACTION ONE FIFTH.  "
"Formally, numeric characters are those with the property value "
"Numeric_Type=Digit, Numeric_Type=Decimal or Numeric_Type=Numeric. For "
"example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2522
msgid ""
">>> '0123456789'.isnumeric()\n"
"True\n"
">>> '٠١٢٣٤٥٦٧٨٩'.isnumeric()  # Arabic-Indic digits zero to nine\n"
"True\n"
">>> '⅕'.isnumeric()  # Vulgar fraction one fifth\n"
"True\n"
">>> '²'.isdecimal(), '²'.isdigit(),  '²'.isnumeric()\n"
"(False, True, True)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2533
msgid "See also :meth:`isdecimal` and :meth:`isdigit`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2538
msgid ""
"Return ``True`` if all characters in the string are printable, ``False`` "
"if it contains at least one non-printable character."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2541
msgid ""
"Here \"printable\" means the character is suitable for :func:`repr` to "
"use in its output; \"non-printable\" means that :func:`repr` on built-in "
"types will hex-escape the character.  It has no bearing on the handling "
"of strings written to :data:`sys.stdout` or :data:`sys.stderr`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2546
msgid ""
"The printable characters are those which in the Unicode character "
"database (see :mod:`unicodedata`) have a general category in group "
"Letter, Mark, Number, Punctuation, or Symbol (L, M, N, P, or S); plus the"
" ASCII space 0x20. Nonprintable characters are those in group Separator "
"or Other (Z or C), except the ASCII space."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2554
msgid ""
">>> ''.isprintable(), ' '.isprintable()\n"
"(True, True)\n"
">>> '\\t'.isprintable(), '\\n'.isprintable()\n"
"(False, False)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2561
msgid "See also :meth:`isspace`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2566
msgid ""
"Return ``True`` if there are only whitespace characters in the string and"
" there is at least one character, ``False`` otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2571
msgid ""
">>> ''.isspace()\n"
"False\n"
">>> ' '.isspace()\n"
"True\n"
">>> '\\t\\n'.isspace() # TAB and BREAK LINE\n"
"True\n"
">>> '\\u3000'.isspace() # IDEOGRAPHIC SPACE\n"
"True"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2582
msgid ""
"A character is *whitespace* if in the Unicode character database (see "
":mod:`unicodedata`), either its general category is ``Zs`` (\"Separator, "
"space\"), or its bidirectional class is one of ``WS``, ``B``, or ``S``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2587
msgid "See also :meth:`isprintable`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2592
msgid ""
"Return ``True`` if the string is a titlecased string and there is at "
"least one character, for example uppercase characters may only follow "
"uncased characters and lowercase characters only cased ones.  Return "
"``False`` otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2598
msgid ""
">>> 'Spam, Spam, Spam'.istitle()\n"
"True\n"
">>> 'spam, spam, spam'.istitle()\n"
"False\n"
">>> 'SPAM, SPAM, SPAM'.istitle()\n"
"False"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2612
msgid ""
"Return ``True`` if all cased characters [4]_ in the string are uppercase "
"and there is at least one cased character, ``False`` otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2630
msgid ""
"Return a string which is the concatenation of the strings in *iterable*. "
"A :exc:`TypeError` will be raised if there are any non-string values in "
"*iterable*, including :class:`bytes` objects.  The separator between "
"elements is the string providing this method. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2635
msgid ""
">>> ', '.join(['spam', 'spam', 'spam'])\n"
"'spam, spam, spam'\n"
">>> '-'.join('Python')\n"
"'P-y-t-h-o-n'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2642
msgid "See also :meth:`split`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2647
msgid ""
"Return the string left justified in a string of length *width*. Padding "
"is done using the specified *fillchar* (default is an ASCII space). The "
"original string is returned if *width* is less than or equal to "
"``len(s)``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2653
msgid ""
">>> 'Python'.ljust(10)\n"
"'Python    '\n"
">>> 'Python'.ljust(10, '.')\n"
"'Python....'\n"
">>> 'Monty Python'.ljust(10, '.')\n"
"'Monty Python'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2662 ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3209
msgid "See also :meth:`rjust`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2667
msgid ""
"Return a copy of the string with all the cased characters [4]_ converted "
"to lowercase. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2670
msgid ""
">>> 'Lower Method Example'.lower()\n"
"'lower method example'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2675
msgid ""
"The lowercasing algorithm used is `described in section 3.13.2 'Default "
"Case Conversion' of the Unicode Standard "
"<https://www.unicode.org/versions/Unicode17.0.0/core-"
"spec/chapter-3/#G34078>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2682
msgid ""
"Return a copy of the string with leading characters removed.  The *chars*"
" argument is a string specifying the set of characters to be removed.  If"
" omitted or ``None``, the *chars* argument defaults to removing "
"whitespace.  The *chars* argument is not a prefix; rather, all "
"combinations of its values are stripped::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2687
msgid ""
">>> '   spacious   '.lstrip()\n"
"'spacious   '\n"
">>> 'www.example.com'.lstrip('cmowz.')\n"
"'example.com'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2692
msgid ""
"See :meth:`str.removeprefix` for a method that will remove a single "
"prefix string rather than all of a set of characters.  For example::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2695
msgid ""
">>> 'Arthur: three!'.lstrip('Arthur: ')\n"
"'ee!'\n"
">>> 'Arthur: three!'.removeprefix('Arthur: ')\n"
"'three!'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2704
msgid ""
"This static method returns a translation table usable for "
":meth:`str.translate`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2706
msgid ""
"If there is only one argument, it must be a dictionary mapping Unicode "
"ordinals (integers) or characters (strings of length 1) to Unicode "
"ordinals, strings (of arbitrary lengths) or ``None``.  Character keys "
"will then be converted to ordinals."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2711
msgid ""
"If there are two arguments, they must be strings of equal length, and in "
"the resulting dictionary, each character in *from* will be mapped to the "
"character at the same position in *to*.  If there is a third argument, it"
" must be a string, whose characters will be mapped to ``None`` in the "
"result."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2718
msgid "*dict* can now be a :class:`frozendict`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2723
msgid ""
"Split the string at the first occurrence of *sep*, and return a 3-tuple "
"containing the part before the separator, the separator itself, and the "
"part after the separator.  If the separator is not found, return a "
"3-tuple containing the string itself, followed by two empty strings."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2730
msgid ""
">>> 'Monty Python'.partition(' ')\n"
"('Monty', ' ', 'Python')\n"
">>> \"Monty Python's Flying Circus\".partition(' ')\n"
"('Monty', ' ', \"Python's Flying Circus\")\n"
">>> 'Monty Python'.partition('-')\n"
"('Monty Python', '', '')"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2739
msgid "See also :meth:`rpartition`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2744
msgid ""
"If the string starts with the *prefix* string, return "
"``string[len(prefix):]``. Otherwise, return a copy of the original "
"string:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2748
msgid ""
">>> 'TestHook'.removeprefix('Test')\n"
"'Hook'\n"
">>> 'BaseTestCase'.removeprefix('Test')\n"
"'BaseTestCase'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2757
msgid "See also :meth:`removesuffix` and :meth:`startswith`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2762
msgid ""
"If the string ends with the *suffix* string and that *suffix* is not "
"empty, return ``string[:-len(suffix)]``. Otherwise, return a copy of the "
"original string:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2766
msgid ""
">>> 'MiscTests'.removesuffix('Tests')\n"
"'Misc'\n"
">>> 'TmpDirMixin'.removesuffix('Tests')\n"
"'TmpDirMixin'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2775
msgid "See also :meth:`removeprefix` and :meth:`endswith`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2780
msgid ""
"Return a copy of the string with all occurrences of substring *old* "
"replaced by *new*.  If *count* is given, only the first *count* "
"occurrences are replaced. If *count* is not specified or ``-1``, then all"
" occurrences are replaced. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2785
msgid ""
">>> 'spam, spam, spam'.replace('spam', 'eggs')\n"
"'eggs, eggs, eggs'\n"
">>> 'spam, spam, spam'.replace('spam', 'eggs', 1)\n"
"'eggs, spam, spam'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2792 ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:4088
msgid "*count* is now supported as a keyword argument."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2798
msgid ""
"Return the highest index in the string where substring *sub* is found, "
"such that *sub* is contained within ``s[start:end]``.  Optional arguments"
" *start* and *end* are interpreted as in slice notation.  Return ``-1`` "
"on failure. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2803
msgid ""
">>> 'spam, spam, spam'.rfind('sp')\n"
"12\n"
">>> 'spam, spam, spam'.rfind('sp', 0, 10)\n"
"6"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2810
msgid "See also :meth:`find` and :meth:`rindex`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2815
msgid ""
"Like :meth:`rfind` but raises :exc:`ValueError` when the substring *sub* "
"is not found. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2819
msgid ""
">>> 'spam, spam, spam'.rindex('spam')\n"
"12\n"
">>> 'spam, spam, spam'.rindex('eggs')\n"
"Traceback (most recent call last):\n"
"  File \"<stdin-0>\", line 1, in <module>\n"
"    'spam, spam, spam'.rindex('eggs')\n"
"    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~^^^^^^^^\n"
"ValueError: substring not found"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2830
msgid "See also :meth:`index` and :meth:`find`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2835
msgid ""
"Return the string right justified in a string of length *width*. Padding "
"is done using the specified *fillchar* (default is an ASCII space). The "
"original string is returned if *width* is less than or equal to "
"``len(s)``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2841
msgid ""
">>> 'Python'.rjust(10)\n"
"'    Python'\n"
">>> 'Python'.rjust(10, '.')\n"
"'....Python'\n"
">>> 'Monty Python'.rjust(10, '.')\n"
"'Monty Python'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2850
msgid "See also :meth:`ljust` and :meth:`zfill`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2855
msgid ""
"Split the string at the last occurrence of *sep*, and return a 3-tuple "
"containing the part before the separator, the separator itself, and the "
"part after the separator.  If the separator is not found, return a "
"3-tuple containing two empty strings, followed by the string itself."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2862
msgid ""
">>> 'Monty Python'.rpartition(' ')\n"
"('Monty', ' ', 'Python')\n"
">>> \"Monty Python's Flying Circus\".rpartition(' ')\n"
"(\"Monty Python's Flying\", ' ', 'Circus')\n"
">>> 'Monty Python'.rpartition('-')\n"
"('', '', 'Monty Python')"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2871
msgid "See also :meth:`partition`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2876
msgid ""
"Return a list of the words in the string, using *sep* as the delimiter "
"string. If *maxsplit* is given, at most *maxsplit* splits are done, the "
"*rightmost* ones.  If *sep* is not specified or ``None``, any "
":meth:`whitespace <str.isspace>` string is a separator.  Except for "
"splitting from the right, :meth:`rsplit` behaves like :meth:`split` which"
" is described in detail below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2886
msgid ""
"Return a copy of the string with trailing characters removed.  The "
"*chars* argument is a string specifying the set of characters to be "
"removed.  If omitted or ``None``, the *chars* argument defaults to "
"removing whitespace.  The *chars* argument is not a suffix; rather, all "
"combinations of its values are stripped. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2892
msgid ""
">>> '   spacious   '.rstrip()\n"
"'   spacious'\n"
">>> 'mississippi'.rstrip('ipz')\n"
"'mississ'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2899
msgid ""
"See :meth:`removesuffix` for a method that will remove a single suffix "
"string rather than all of a set of characters.  For example::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2902
msgid ""
">>> 'Monty Python'.rstrip(' Python')\n"
"'M'\n"
">>> 'Monty Python'.removesuffix(' Python')\n"
"'Monty'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2907
msgid "See also :meth:`strip`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2912
msgid ""
"Return a list of the words in the string, using *sep* as the delimiter "
"string.  If *maxsplit* is given, at most *maxsplit* splits are done "
"(thus, the list will have at most ``maxsplit+1`` elements).  If "
"*maxsplit* is not specified or ``-1``, then there is no limit on the "
"number of splits (all possible splits are made)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2918
msgid ""
"If *sep* is given, consecutive delimiters are not grouped together and "
"are deemed to delimit empty strings (for example, ``'1,,2'.split(',')`` "
"returns ``['1', '', '2']``).  The *sep* argument may consist of multiple "
"characters as a single delimiter (to split with multiple delimiters, use "
":func:`re.split`). Splitting an empty string with a specified separator "
"returns ``['']``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2927
msgid ""
">>> '1,2,3'.split(',')\n"
"['1', '2', '3']\n"
">>> '1,2,3'.split(',', maxsplit=1)\n"
"['1', '2,3']\n"
">>> '1,2,,3,'.split(',')\n"
"['1', '2', '', '3', '']\n"
">>> '1<>2<>3<4'.split('<>')\n"
"['1', '2', '3<4']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2938
msgid ""
"If *sep* is not specified or is ``None``, a different splitting algorithm"
" is applied: runs of consecutive :meth:`whitespace <str.isspace>` are "
"regarded as a single separator, and the result will contain no empty "
"strings at the start or end if the string has leading or trailing "
"whitespace.  Consequently, splitting an empty string or a string "
"consisting of just whitespace with a ``None`` separator returns ``[]``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2948
msgid ""
">>> '1 2 3'.split()\n"
"['1', '2', '3']\n"
">>> '1 2 3'.split(maxsplit=1)\n"
"['1', '2 3']\n"
">>> '   1   2   3   '.split()\n"
"['1', '2', '3']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2957
msgid ""
"If *sep* is not specified or is ``None`` and  *maxsplit* is ``0``, only "
"leading runs of consecutive whitespace are considered."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2962
msgid ""
">>> \"\".split(None, 0)\n"
"[]\n"
">>> \"   \".split(None, 0)\n"
"[]\n"
">>> \"   foo   \".split(maxsplit=0)\n"
"['foo   ']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2971
msgid "See also :meth:`join` and :meth:`rsplit`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2979
msgid ""
"Return a list of the lines in the string, breaking at line boundaries.  "
"Line breaks are not included in the resulting list unless *keepends* is "
"given and true."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2983
msgid ""
"This method splits on the following line boundaries.  In particular, the "
"boundaries are a superset of :term:`universal newlines`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2987
msgid "Representation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2987
msgid "Description"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2989
msgid "``\\n``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2989
msgid "Line Feed"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2991
msgid "``\\r``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2991
msgid "Carriage Return"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2993
msgid "``\\r\\n``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2993
msgid "Carriage Return + Line Feed"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2995
msgid "``\\v`` or ``\\x0b``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2995
msgid "Line Tabulation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2997
msgid "``\\f`` or ``\\x0c``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2997
msgid "Form Feed"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2999
msgid "``\\x1c``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:2999
msgid "File Separator"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3001
msgid "``\\x1d``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3001
msgid "Group Separator"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3003
msgid "``\\x1e``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3003
msgid "Record Separator"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3005
msgid "``\\x85``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3005
msgid "Next Line (C1 Control Code)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3007
msgid "``\\u2028``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3007
msgid "Line Separator"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3009
msgid "``\\u2029``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3009
msgid "Paragraph Separator"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3014
msgid "``\\v`` and ``\\f`` added to list of line boundaries."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3016 ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3122
#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3299 ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:4304
#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:4323 ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:4414
#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:4430 ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:4455
#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:4469 ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:4497
#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:4511 ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:4529
#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:4556 ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:4579
#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:4606 ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:4648
#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:4672
msgid "For example::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3018
msgid ""
">>> 'ab c\\n\\nde fg\\rkl\\r\\n'.splitlines()\n"
"['ab c', '', 'de fg', 'kl']\n"
">>> 'ab c\\n\\nde fg\\rkl\\r\\n'.splitlines(keepends=True)\n"
"['ab c\\n', '\\n', 'de fg\\r', 'kl\\r\\n']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3023
msgid ""
"Unlike :meth:`~str.split` when a delimiter string *sep* is given, this "
"method returns an empty list for the empty string, and a terminal line "
"break does not result in an extra line::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3027
msgid ""
">>> \"\".splitlines()\n"
"[]\n"
">>> \"One line\\n\".splitlines()\n"
"['One line']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3032
msgid "For comparison, ``split('\\n')`` gives::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3034
msgid ""
">>> ''.split('\\n')\n"
"['']\n"
">>> 'Two lines\\n'.split('\\n')\n"
"['Two lines', '']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3042
msgid ""
"Return ``True`` if string starts with the *prefix*, otherwise return "
"``False``. *prefix* can also be a tuple of prefixes to look for.  With "
"optional *start*, test string beginning at that position.  With optional "
"*end*, stop comparing string at that position."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3049
msgid ""
">>> 'Python'.startswith('Py')\n"
"True\n"
">>> 'a tuple of prefixes'.startswith(('at', 'a'))\n"
"True\n"
">>> 'Python is amazing'.startswith('is', 7)\n"
"True"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3058
msgid "See also :meth:`endswith` and :meth:`removeprefix`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3063
msgid ""
"Return a copy of the string with the leading and trailing characters "
"removed. The *chars* argument is a string specifying the set of "
"characters to be removed. If omitted or ``None``, the *chars* argument "
"defaults to removing whitespace. The *chars* argument is not a prefix or "
"suffix; rather, all combinations of its values are stripped."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3069
msgid "Whitespace characters are defined by :meth:`str.isspace`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3073
msgid ""
">>> '   spacious   '.strip()\n"
"'spacious'\n"
">>> 'www.example.com'.strip('cmowz.')\n"
"'example'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3080
msgid ""
"The outermost leading and trailing *chars* argument values are stripped "
"from the string. Characters are removed from the leading end until "
"reaching a string character that is not contained in the set of "
"characters in *chars*. A similar action takes place on the trailing end."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3087
msgid ""
">>> comment_string = '#....... Section 3.2.1 Issue #32 .......'\n"
">>> comment_string.strip('.#! ')\n"
"'Section 3.2.1 Issue #32'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3093
msgid "See also :meth:`rstrip`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3098
msgid ""
"Return a copy of the string with uppercase characters converted to "
"lowercase and vice versa. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3101
msgid ""
">>> 'Hello World'.swapcase()\n"
"'hELLO wORLD'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3106
msgid ""
"Note that it is not necessarily true that ``s.swapcase().swapcase() == "
"s``. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3109
msgid ""
">>> 'straße'.swapcase().swapcase()\n"
"'strasse'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3114
msgid "See also :meth:`str.lower` and :meth:`str.upper`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3119
msgid ""
"Return a titlecased version of the string where words start with an "
"uppercase character and the remaining characters are lowercase."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3124
msgid ""
">>> 'Hello world'.title()\n"
"'Hello World'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3127 ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:4616
msgid ""
"The algorithm uses a simple language-independent definition of a word as "
"groups of consecutive letters.  The definition works in many contexts but"
" it means that apostrophes in contractions and possessives form word "
"boundaries, which may not be the desired result::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3132
msgid ""
">>> \"they're bill's friends from the UK\".title()\n"
"\"They'Re Bill'S Friends From The Uk\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3135
msgid ""
"The :func:`string.capwords` function does not have this problem, as it "
"splits words on spaces only."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3138
msgid ""
"Alternatively, a workaround for apostrophes can be constructed using "
"regular expressions::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3141
msgid ""
">>> import re\n"
">>> def titlecase(s):\n"
"...     return re.sub(r\"[A-Za-z]+('[A-Za-z]+)?\",\n"
"...                   lambda mo: mo.group(0).capitalize(),\n"
"...                   s)\n"
"...\n"
">>> titlecase(\"they're bill's friends.\")\n"
"\"They're Bill's Friends.\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3150
msgid "See also :meth:`istitle` and :meth:`capitalize`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3155
msgid ""
"Return a copy of the string in which each character has been mapped "
"through the given translation table.  The table must be an object that "
"implements indexing via :meth:`~object.__getitem__`, typically a "
":term:`mapping` or :term:`sequence`.  When indexed by a Unicode ordinal "
"(an integer), the table object can do any of the following: return a "
"Unicode ordinal or a string, to map the character to one or more other "
"characters; return ``None``, to delete the character from the return "
"string; or raise a :exc:`LookupError` exception, to map the character to "
"itself."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3164
msgid ""
"You can use :meth:`str.maketrans` to create a translation map from "
"character-to-character mappings in different formats."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3167
msgid ""
"The following example uses a mapping to replace ``'a'`` with ``'X'``, "
"``'b'`` with ``'Y'``, and delete ``'c'``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3170
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
">>> 'abc123'.translate({ord('a'): 'X', ord('b'): 'Y', ord('c'): None})\n"
"'XY123'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3175
msgid ""
"See also the :mod:`codecs` module for a more flexible approach to custom "
"character mappings."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3181
msgid ""
"Return a copy of the string with all the cased characters [4]_ converted "
"to uppercase.  Note that ``s.upper().isupper()`` might be ``False`` if "
"``s`` contains no cased characters or if the Unicode category of the "
"resulting character(s) is not \"Lu\" (Letter, uppercase), but e.g. \"Lt\""
" (Letter, titlecase)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3187
msgid ""
"The uppercasing algorithm used is `described in section 3.13.2 'Default "
"Case Conversion' of the Unicode Standard "
"<https://www.unicode.org/versions/Unicode17.0.0/core-"
"spec/chapter-3/#G34078>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3194
msgid ""
"Return a copy of the string left filled with ASCII ``'0'`` digits to make"
" a string of length *width*. A leading sign prefix (``'+'``/``'-'``) is "
"handled by inserting the padding *after* the sign character rather than "
"before. The original string is returned if *width* is less than or equal "
"to ``len(s)``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3202
msgid ""
">>> \"42\".zfill(5)\n"
"'00042'\n"
">>> \"-42\".zfill(5)\n"
"'-0042'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3229
msgid "Formatted String Literals (f-strings)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3232
msgid ""
"The :keyword:`await` and :keyword:`async for` can be used in expressions "
"within f-strings."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3235
msgid "Added the debug specifier (``=``)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3237
msgid ""
"Many restrictions on expressions within f-strings have been removed. "
"Notably, nested strings, comments, and backslashes are now permitted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3241
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"An :dfn:`f-string` (formally a :dfn:`formatted string literal`) is a "
"string literal that is prefixed with ``f`` or ``F``. This type of string "
"literal allows embedding the results of arbitrary Python expressions "
"within *replacement fields*, which are delimited by curly brackets "
"(``{}``). Each replacement field must contain an expression, optionally "
"followed by:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3248
msgid "a *debug specifier* -- an equal sign (``=``);"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3249
msgid "a *conversion specifier* -- ``!s``, ``!r`` or ``!a``; and/or"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3250
msgid "a *format specifier* prefixed with a colon (``:``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3252
msgid ""
"See the :ref:`Lexical Analysis section on f-strings <f-strings>` for "
"details on the syntax of these fields."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3256
msgid "Debug specifier"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3260
msgid ""
"If a debug specifier -- an equal sign (``=``) -- appears after the "
"replacement field expression, the resulting f-string will contain the "
"expression's source, the equal sign, and the value of the expression. "
"This is often useful for debugging::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3265
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
">>> number = 14.3\n"
">>> f'{number=}'\n"
"'number=14.3'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3269
msgid ""
"Whitespace before, inside and after the expression, as well as whitespace"
" after the equal sign, is significant --- it is retained in the result::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3272
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
">>> f'{ number  -  4  = }'\n"
"' number  -  4  = 10.3'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3277
msgid "Conversion specifier"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3279
msgid ""
"By default, the value of a replacement field expression is converted to a"
" string using :func:`str`::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3282
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
">>> from fractions import Fraction\n"
">>> one_third = Fraction(1, 3)\n"
">>> f'{one_third}'\n"
"'1/3'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3287
msgid ""
"When a debug specifier but no format specifier is used, the default "
"conversion instead uses :func:`repr`::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3290
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
">>> f'{one_third = }'\n"
"'one_third = Fraction(1, 3)'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3293
msgid "The conversion can be specified explicitly using one of these specifiers:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3295
msgid "``!s`` for :func:`str`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3296
msgid "``!r`` for :func:`repr`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3297
msgid "``!a`` for :func:`ascii`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3301
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
">>> str(one_third)\n"
"'1/3'\n"
">>> repr(one_third)\n"
"'Fraction(1, 3)'\n"
"\n"
">>> f'{one_third!s} is {one_third!r}'\n"
"'1/3 is Fraction(1, 3)'\n"
"\n"
">>> string = \"¡kočka 😸!\"\n"
">>> ascii(string)\n"
"\"'\\\\xa1ko\\\\u010dka \\\\U0001f638!'\"\n"
"\n"
">>> f'{string = !a}'\n"
"\"string = '\\\\xa1ko\\\\u010dka \\\\U0001f638!'\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3318
msgid "Format specifier"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3320
msgid ""
"After the expression has been evaluated, and possibly converted using an "
"explicit conversion specifier, it is formatted using the :func:`format` "
"function. If the replacement field includes a *format specifier* "
"introduced by a colon (``:``), the specifier is passed to :func:`!format`"
" as the second argument. The result of :func:`!format` is then used as "
"the final value for the replacement field. For example::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3327
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
">>> from fractions import Fraction\n"
">>> one_third = Fraction(1, 3)\n"
">>> f'{one_third:.6f}'\n"
"'0.333333'\n"
">>> f'{one_third:_^+10}'\n"
"'___+1/3___'\n"
">>> f'{one_third!r:_^20}'\n"
"'___Fraction(1, 3)___'\n"
">>> f'{one_third = :~>10}~'\n"
"'one_third = ~~~~~~~1/3~'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3341
msgid "Template String Literals (t-strings)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3343
msgid ""
"A :dfn:`t-string` (formally a :dfn:`template string literal`) is a string"
" literal that is prefixed with ``t`` or ``T``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3346
msgid ""
"These strings follow the same syntax and evaluation rules as "
":ref:`formatted string literals <stdtypes-fstrings>`, with the following "
"differences:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3350
msgid ""
"Rather than evaluating to a ``str`` object, template string literals "
"evaluate to a :class:`string.templatelib.Template` object."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3353
msgid ""
"The :func:`format` protocol is not used. Instead, the format specifier "
"and conversions (if any) are passed to a new "
":class:`~string.templatelib.Interpolation` object that is created for "
"each evaluated expression. It is up to code that processes the resulting "
":class:`~string.templatelib.Template` object to decide how to handle "
"format specifiers and conversions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3360
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Format specifiers containing nested replacement fields are evaluated "
"eagerly, prior to being passed to the "
":class:`~string.templatelib.Interpolation` object. For instance, an "
"interpolation of the form ``{amount:.{precision}f}`` will evaluate the "
"inner expression ``{precision}`` to determine the value of the "
"``format_spec`` attribute. If ``precision`` were to be ``2``, the "
"resulting format specifier would be ``'.2f'``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3368
msgid ""
"When the equals sign ``'='`` is provided in an interpolation expression, "
"the text of the expression is appended to the literal string that "
"precedes the relevant interpolation. This includes the equals sign and "
"any surrounding whitespace. The :class:`!Interpolation` instance for the "
"expression will be created as normal, except that "
":attr:`~string.templatelib.Interpolation.conversion` will be set to "
"'``r``' (:func:`repr`) by default. If an explicit conversion or format "
"specifier is provided, this will override the default behaviour."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3382
msgid "``printf``-style String Formatting"
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3395
msgid ""
"The formatting operations described here exhibit a variety of quirks that"
" lead to a number of common errors (such as failing to display tuples and"
" dictionaries correctly)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3399
msgid ""
"Using :ref:`formatted string literals <f-strings>`, the "
":meth:`str.format` interface, or :class:`string.Template` may help avoid "
"these errors. Each of these alternatives provides their own trade-offs "
"and benefits of simplicity, flexibility, and/or extensibility."
msgstr ""

#: ../../builtins/stdtypes.rst:3404
msgid ""
"String objects have one unique built-in operation: the ``%`` operator "
"(modulo). This is also known as the string *formatting* or "
"*interpolation* operator. Given ``format % values`` (where *format* is a "
"string), ``%`` conversion specifications in *format* are replaced with "
"zero or more elements of *values*. The effect is similar to using the "
":c:func:`sprintf` function in the C language. For example:"
msgstr ""

